{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0028", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$2$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:b15315ec3c2fa7b154c0d17c24b63d47f12b572ea3fa602a460510e1a685b882", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:4b684aae84697b70fa019191fadbf4177eb39836e7fd7cd2f3e0d19cc5286d6e", "source_text": "As in the case of the fibre products above, equalizers when they exist are unique up to unique isomorphism. There is a straightforward generalization of this definition to the case where we have more than <MATH_0001> morphisms.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e25faef882d0de132dca7410a62de67dbb329c55c0465b08cfab0c439cc05b60", "unit_id": "tag:0028:p001"}
